@title Time Attack - reach a live Time Attack lap, then drive (the line is STALE)
@game  PCSE00001
@shot-every 200

# ============================================================================
# >>> THE DRIVING LINE NO LONGER DRIVES THE LAP. MEASURED 2026-08-24.
# The boot ladder below is CORRECT and still is: the run reaches Lap Time Attack
# with the car on the start line at about f12000 (HUD `LAP 1/30`, `000 mph`,
# TOTAL TIME blank), and drives cleanly from there - f13000 is 069 mph on the
# racing line at 00:12.583, f14000 is 078 mph through the tunnel at 00:29.250,
# both with the minimap marker advancing.
#
# It comes apart between f14000 and f14500: at f14500 the car is sideways
# against a barrier at 00:37.583, and by f15000 the HUD reads `WRONG WAY!!` with
# the car stopped in scenery. NO LAP IS COMPLETED, and the two-lap claim this
# header used to make is simply false now - it is stated here rather than left
# for the next reader to discover from a screenshot.
#
# The line is OPEN LOOP - a recorded response, not a controller - so any change
# to how much simulation a counted frame buys makes it drift, and drift only has
# to win once. What to do about it, and why it is not a small edit: the line was
# authored by `working-area/drive.py`, a greedy 34-candidate search through
# `explore` with a 150-frame window, resumable from `working-area/race-line.json`.
# Every candidate replays the whole prefix, so a step near f14000 costs about
# three batches of a 14,000-frame boot - roughly a quarter of an hour - and the
# remaining ~32 steps to f19000 are the better part of a day of wall clock. It
# needs to be STARTED and left, not squeezed into a session.
#
# The frame to resume from is f14150: the last window that is still clean.
#
# WHAT THIS RECIPE DID WHEN IT WAS AUTHORED
# Walked the mandatory first-run wizard, entered Lap Time Attack, and then DROVE
# the circuit: two complete laps, confirmed by the game's own HUD reading
# `LAP 3/30` with two finished lap times listed at the end of the run.
#
# HOW IT WAS AUTHORED (lessons for the next agent)
# - Judge this title ONLY through `vitaslop-desktop --headless`. The session's
#   software renderer does draw the race (HUD included), but the GPU path is the
#   one a player sees and the two do NOT agree - see the render note below.
# - The driving line is not hand-authored. It is the recorded response of a
#   closed-loop controller that steered at the far end of the ROAD MATERIAL
#   ahead, re-measured every 10 frames, with a LAP DIRECTION carried across the
#   whole run so a spin cannot turn the lap around. Every value here is a
#   response the car actually gave.
# - WHY LAP DIRECTION MATTERS, because it cost two earlier attempts: "the far
#   end of the road ahead" is just as true of the road already driven. Once the
#   car is pointing backwards, a direction-agnostic controller aims at the start
#   straight and drives a fast lap the WRONG WAY, which looks like progress to
#   every measurement except the game's own lap counter.
# - A COUNTED FRAME IS NOT 1/60 s OF GAME TIME here - but the RATIO HAS MOVED,
#   which is the whole reason the driving line above is stale. When the line was
#   recorded the guest advanced about 5.3 frames of simulation per frame this
#   recipe counts. MEASURED 2026-08-24 off the game's own TOTAL TIME (00:12.583
#   at f13000, 00:29.250 at f14000): a thousand counted frames now buy 16.667
#   seconds, which is a thousand simulation frames - a ratio of ONE. Frame-keyed
#   timings below are on the counted clock; do not convert them to seconds, and
#   do not trust a converted figure written before this line.
#
# WHAT IS NOT ASSERTED, and why
# There is no @assert, because the lap counter's address is not yet found. Do
# NOT re-try these - they are measured dead ends:
#   * scanning u32 for the displayed lap value across a lap boundary: the
#     survivors are noise, and none of 67 candidates moved the HUD when poked.
#   * 0x81536714 / 0x815367d4 (+0x18 in the two racer records whose +0x00 holds
#     the lap TOTAL 30): they read 3 and 2 at a frame where the HUD reads LAP 1,
#     so they are constants, not counters.
#   * poking as a test at all: the HUD caches the lap number and only re-reads
#     it on a lap event, so a poke that does not move the display proves
#     nothing either way.
# The reliable next move is `@watch` on a candidate plus a driven lap boundary,
# reading the HUD from a shot at the same frame.
#
# RENDER DEFECTS: NONE LEFT IN THIS RUN, as of 2026-08-24.
# This block used to list three - the player's car as saturated red/black blobs,
# large trackside objects as untextured white boxes, and RGB fringing whenever
# the camera came close to geometry. All three are GONE: the f12000 start-line
# frame renders the car's paint, decals and glass correctly, the trackside
# signage is textured, and the run reports ZERO shader fallbacks (`grep "FALLS
# BACK"` finds nothing over 19,500 frames). A defect list that outlives its
# defects reads as a known-bad renderer and stops people looking, so it is
# emptied here rather than left standing.
# ============================================================================

